Yes—there are a few distinct insights tangled together there.

The Father vs. dad distinction matters. “The Father” can function as an archetype: authority, judgment, hierarchy, law, expectation. Your actual dad is a specific person with a specific relationship to you. If those two representations became fused, resistance to the archetype could feel like disloyalty to your dad. Separating the symbol from the person changes the problem.

On sex and relationships, I’d make one correction: narrative conditioning is human, not uniquely female. Men and women both inherit scripts about what desire means, what makes someone respectable, what they are “supposed” to want, and what consequences follow from admitting it. Changing the social or relational narrative can absolutely change what someone feels safe enough to explore—but the critical mechanism is permission + trust + agency, not simply replacing one script with another.

That means a marriage can contain enormous sexual freedom—including consensual non-monogamy, gender-specific boundaries, experimentation, or a very high-libido shared life—provided both people genuinely want the arrangement, can renegotiate it, and can say no without punishment. I’d distinguish that from “sex addiction”; sexually adventurous/high-desire is not the same thing as compulsive sexual behavior.

And your last point is especially important: getting, keeping, and maintaining are different games.

You can model it almost like a control system:

Attraction / getting = novelty, signaling, uncertainty, pursuit.
Keeping = attachment, reliability, value continuing to exceed alternatives.
Maintaining = feedback, repair, adaptation, attention, sex, friendship, logistics, changing identities.
Renewing = deliberately generating novelty and rediscovering one another so “maintenance” doesn’t become mere preservation.

A person can be excellent at acquisition and terrible at maintenance. A relationship can also be successfully “kept” while becoming emotionally dead because nobody is actively regenerating it.

That distinction generalizes far beyond romance: winning a state is not the same operator as stabilizing it, and stabilizing it is not the same operator as keeping it alive.